              "text": "Here is a brand-new idiom for the modern age:\n\n### **\"To iron a wet sponge\"**\n\n***\n\n### **Spilling the Beans (What it means and how to use it):**\n\n**Definition:** \nTo waste an immense amount of time and energy trying to force rigid structure, flatness, or perfection onto something that is inherently fluid, chaotic, or meant to be flexible. \n\n**The Origin/Visual:**\nPicture it: You have a big, squishy, wet cleaning sponge. For some reason, you want it to be perfectly flat, crisp, and orderly, so you grab a hot clothing iron and press down. What happens? You get a face full of scalding steam, a terrible screeching hiss, a ruined iron, and the moment you lift the iron, the sponge just springs right back to its squishy shape. It is a completely futile, frustrating, and slightly dangerous task. \n\n**When to use it:**\nYou use this idiom when someone\u2014usually a micromanager, an over-thinker, or a perfectionist\u2014is trying to apply strict, bureaucratic rules to something that needs to be organic or creative. \n\n*   *Example 1 (Workplace):* \"Our manager is trying to make us log our creative brainstorming sessions on a minute-by-minute spreadsheet. He\u2019s really **ironing a wet sponge** on this project.\"\n*   *Example 2 (Parenting/Life):* \"I tried to plan a strict, hourly itinerary for our family vacation with three toddlers, but I quickly realized I was just **ironing a wet sponge**.\"\n\nIn short, when you \"iron a wet sponge,\" you aren't fixing the problem; you're just making a lot of useless steam and burning your own fingers!",
